  {
   "id": "EYzLRi1lAwAJ",
   "from": "Jeffrey Doser",
   "author": "0b274a0ee3490d75",
   "date": "2025-01-20T14:03:18Z",
   "subject": "Re: Issue with scaling(?) and mapping predictions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Hi Katie, <br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for sending the data\r\n offline! After looking at the code and output, it looks to me like the \r\napproach where you use mean() and sd() to standardize the covariates for\r\n prediction is actually giving you the correct results, and there isn&#39;t \r\nany problem with conversions from rasters to data frames. Looking at \r\nyour model output, you do have a significant positive linear effect of \r\nforest cover, but notice also that you have a significant positive <i>quadratic</i>\r\n effect. This corresponds to the model estimating a &quot;U&quot; shaped \r\nrelationship with forest cover, where occupancy probability peaks at low\r\n values and then at high values, so there is not just an estimated \r\npositive linear relationship with forest cover. So, given those results,\r\n the results of the first graph (that uses the standardization approach I\r\n outline in the vignette) actually gives results that line up with what \r\nthe output of the model suggests. <br></div><div><br></div><div>When you\r\n use scale() in &quot;occ.formula&quot; when fitting the model, the covariates are\r\n scaled by the mean and standard deviation of the covariate values at \r\nthe observed locations. When doing this, any predictions you \r\nsubsequently do must scale the covariates by the same values, otherwise \r\nthe covariates will be on different scales. In your case, the mean of \r\nthe covariates of forest cover at observed locations is about 0.96 with \r\nan sd of 0.11, while the mean of forest cover at prediction locations is\r\n about 0.73 with an sd of 0.36. Notice that these are different from the\r\n observed values. When you just use scale() on the prediction locations,\r\n this standardizes the values with the prediction means and sd, which \r\nare different from the observed values, and thus the observed covariates\r\n and prediction covariates aren&#39;t on the same scales and the results \r\nfrom such a prediction aren&#39;t valid. <br></div><div><br></div><div>Let me know if I can clarify any of this. <br></div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div><br></div><div>Jeff</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Friday, January 17, 2025 at 10:49:04 AM UTC-5 Jeffrey Doser wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hi Katie, <br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for the question. If you could send some code/data so that I can reproduce the problem that would be very helpful to try and gauge what&#39;s going on here. If all is done correctly, scale() and the mean &amp; SD approach should give the same results, but there could be some reason as to why it&#39;s not working with your example (particularly if you&#39;re working with actual raster objects as opposed to data frames or matrices). Feel free to send a reproducible example here or send it to me offline (<a rel=\"nofollow\">jwd...@ncsu.edu</a>). <br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div><br></div><div>Jeff<br></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Thursday, January 16, 2025 at 7:39:05 AM UTC-5 <a rel=\"nofollow\">katiesp...@hotmail.co.uk</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\">Hi All,</div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><br></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\">I&#39;m using the spOccupancy package to fit a single-species occupancy model (PGOcc function) to assess sun bear occupancy across Borneo. In line with our expectations, the model outputs indicate that forest cover and human population pressure are influential determinants of sun bear occurrence - sun bears are more likely to occur in forested areas free from human influence. </div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><br></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\">However, issues arise when attempting to map spatial predictions of sun bear occurrence values across a wider geographic area. Despite identifying a positive relationship with forest cover, deforested areas (cells with a proportion of 0 to 0.3 forest cover) are predicted to have the highest occupancy for sun bears (psi=0.999).</div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><br></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\">Based on my initial attempts to diagnose the problem, this issue seems to relate to how the raster&#39;s used in the spatial predictions are scaled. When I follow the online tutorial [<a href=\"https://doserlab.com/files/spoccupancy-web/articles/modelfitting\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://doserlab.com/files/spoccupancy-web/articles/modelfitting&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792464158618000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2YYBxrqBfWgQSIEAJGLjzb\">https://doserlab.com/files/spoccupancy-web/articles/modelfitting</a>], by scaling rasters using the mean and standard deviation derived from the observed covariate values across sampling locations (<span style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,0.87);font-family:Roboto,RobotoDraft,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">(forest21_df$val - mean(data.ssom$occ.covs$mean_forest)) /</span><span style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,0.87);font-family:Roboto,RobotoDraft,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,0.87);font-family:Roboto,RobotoDraft,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,0.87);font-family:Roboto,RobotoDraft,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">sd(data.ssom$occ.covs$mean_forest))</span><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">, spatial predictions derived from the predict function become unreliable in deforested areas and seem to follow the opposite trend depicted on the caterpillar plots of covariate associations (MCMCplot(out$beta.samples, ref_ovl = TRUE, ci = c(75, 95)))). </span></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"><br></span></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">However, if I scale the same rasters using R&#39;s scale function (forest</span><b style=\"color:rgba(0,0,0,0.87);font-family:Roboto,RobotoDraft,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px\">21.0 &lt;- scale(forest21_df$val)</b><span style=\"font-size:12pt\"> (as I did for the covariates in the initial model), the predict function produces a map that more accurately reflects the observed relationship between occurrence and forest cover - though I&#39;m not entirely sure this is the correct approach. </span></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><br></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">I have a feeling this could be due to my forest values in my data being skewed towards 1 (as camera-traps are typically placed in forested areas).</span><span style=\"font-size:12pt\">  </span></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-feature-settings:inherit;margin:0px;padding:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:black\">Any advice you could offer to diagnose this issue would be much appreciated. I&#39;m not sure if I&#39;m overlooking something in my code or if this reflects a broader underlying issue. <span style=\"font-size:12pt\">Please let me know if a more reproducible example is needed.</span></div><div style=\"border:0px;font-variant-numeric:inherit;font-variant-east-asian:inherit;font-variant-alternates:inherit;font-stretch:inherit;font-size:12pt;line-height:inherit;font-family:Aptos,Aptos_EmbeddedFont,Aptos_MSFontService,Calibri,Helvetica,sans-serif,serif,EmojiFont;font-size-adjust:inherit;font-kerning:inherit;font-f